<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Zhen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1449">1449</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Zhen was a Chinese court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> who, as tutor and adviser to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhengtong-emperor">Zhengtong Emperor</a>, became the first eunuch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a> to wield significant political power, exercising dictatorial control over the court in the 1440s. Educated in the palace school for eunuchs founded in 1426, he joined the Directorate of Ceremonial in 1435, and after the death of Grand Empress Dowager Zhang in 1442 and the retirement of the elderly grand secretaries no official could check him; he imprisoned opponents and in some cases brought about their deaths. In 1449 he was killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tumu-crisis">Tumu Crisis</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> defeated the army he had urged the emperor to lead and captured the emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-zhen-eunuch.jpg" alt="Wang Zhen" width="330" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:太监王振画像.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A few rose to rule the court, as <b>Wang Zhen</b>, Liu Jin and Wei Zhongxian did.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the influence of the eunuch <b>Wang Zhen</b>, the Ming emperor Zhengtong led his army against the Oirat Mongols and was defeated and captured at Tumu fortress.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-1">1 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
